---
// Development-only editorial overview, injected by astro.config.mjs.
// Inherit the production post shell and fonts; do not restyle the page template.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import BlogPostLayout from '../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import BibliographyItem from '../components/BibliographyItem.astro';
import checkData from '../../docs/warehouse-audit-checks.json';

const entry = await getEntry('posts', 'warehouse-audit-checklist');
if (!entry) throw new Error('The existing warehouse audit post is required for this overview.');
Astro.response.headers.set('X-Robots-Tag', 'noindex, nofollow');
const frontmatter = {
  ...entry.data,
  title: 'Warehouse Audit Checklist',
  description: 'Draft overview of the warehouse audit article, using the existing post template.',
  readTime: 'Draft overview',
};
const sections = [
  ['warehouse-audit-working-pack', 'Excel and PDF pack (coming soon)'],
  ['what-is-a-warehouse-audit', 'What is a warehouse audit?'],
  ['practical-checklist', 'The Practical Warehouse Audit Checklist'],
  ['plan-the-audit', 'Plan the audit around the decision'],
  ['execute-the-audit', 'Executing the audit: Don’t Boil the Ocean'],
  ['financial-controls', 'Stock discrepancies and financial controls'],
  ['compliance-and-customer-audits', 'Compliance and customer audits'],
  ['findings-and-actions', 'Turn findings into decisions'],
  ['audit-frequency', 'How often should you audit?'],
  ['technology-and-people', 'Technology and the people doing the work'],
  ['references', 'References'],
];
const headings = sections.map(([slug, text]) => ({ depth: 2, slug, text }));
const areas = [...new Set(checkData.checks.map(check => check.area))];
const process = [
  ['Purpose and criteria', 'What decision does this review support, and which requirements apply?'],
  ['Evidence', 'What do the records, observations and worker accounts establish?'],
  ['Finding and uncertainty', 'What differs from the requirement? What remains unexplained?'],
  ['Action or decision', 'Who owns the next step, and what authority or resources do they need?'],
  ['Verification', 'What evidence will show whether the action worked?'],
];
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter} headings={headings}>
  <aside slot="intro" class="mb-8 border-l-2 border-[#fd4f00] pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed" aria-label="Editorial preview note">
    <strong>Article preview.</strong> Illustrations are sketches. The download files and email signup are not available yet. Author and date details are inherited from the current post.
  </aside>

  <p>Operating a warehouse means balancing speed, safety and accuracy. When stock differences keep returning, dispatches fall behind or a customer asks for evidence, a warehouse audit helps you examine what is happening and decide what needs to change. Use this checklist to agree what to review, gather the evidence and give each finding a practical next step.</p>
  <section class="not-prose audit-pack-offer font-recoleta" aria-labelledby="warehouse-audit-working-pack">
    <h2 id="warehouse-audit-working-pack">Excel and PDF audit pack</h2>
    <p>Keep the questions, evidence and follow-up in one working record. We’re preparing an editable Excel workbook, printable PDF floor sheets and completed examples for your team review.</p>
    <p class="audit-pack-status"><strong>Coming soon.</strong> The pack will be included with a DataDocks email subscription. Downloads and email signup are not available yet.</p>
    <details class="audit-pack-preview">
      <summary>Preview pack contents and signup <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg></summary>
      <div class="audit-pack-preview-content">
        <div class="audit-pack-art" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="audit-pack-sheet"><span>PDF</span><strong>Warehouse audit</strong><p>Scope<br />Evidence<br />Actions</p><i></i><i></i></div>
          <div class="audit-pack-workbook"><span>EXCEL</span><strong>A working record<br />for the whole review</strong><div class="audit-sheet-lines"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></div>
        </div>
        <p>The planned pack will bring these 28 checks into an editable working record and printable floor sheets, with examples to help you record findings and assign follow-up.</p>
        <ul class="audit-pack-contents">
          <li><strong>Editable Excel workbook:</strong> plan the review, record evidence and track findings, action owners and verification.</li>
          <li><strong>Printable PDF sheets:</strong> take the checklist onto the warehouse floor and make notes as you review.</li>
          <li><strong>Completed examples and a management review template:</strong> see how a stock investigation and provider review become findings, assigned work and decisions to follow up.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="audit-signup-preview" aria-label="Email signup preview, inactive">
          <label for="audit-preview-email">Email address</label>
          <input id="audit-preview-email" type="email" placeholder="Your email address" disabled />
          <button type="button" disabled>Subscribe &amp; get the audit pack</button>
          <p class="audit-small">When the pack is available, you’ll be able to subscribe to DataDocks emails about warehouse guidance and product updates and receive the Excel and PDF files. Unsubscribe anytime. <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy policy</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2 id="what-is-a-warehouse-audit">What is a warehouse audit?</h2>
  <p>A warehouse audit is a planned review of how a warehouse operates against the requirements it needs to meet. It compares procedures and records with what happens on the floor, then records the findings and the work needed to address them.</p>
  <p>The reason for the review determines its scope. An inventory lead investigating repeated stock differences needs different evidence from a director reviewing a warehouse provider before renewal. Start with the question you need answered, then choose the relevant areas below.</p>


  <figure aria-label="Warehouse work and its supporting evidence">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="warehouse-figure-title warehouse-figure-desc" class="w-full text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">
      <title id="warehouse-figure-title">Warehouse work and its supporting evidence</title>
      <desc id="warehouse-figure-desc">An illustrative warehouse connects to a record sheet and a conversation. It represents physical work, records and review, not a prescribed warehouse layout.</desc>
      <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round">
        <path d="M58 110 193 54 320 106 185 169Z" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity=".04" />
        <path d="M58 110v90l127 42v-73m0 73 135-49v-87" />
        <path d="M83 134v27l27 9v-27zm52 18v27l27 9v-27zm79 24v54l35-13v-54zm63-24v54l24-9v-54z" />
        <path d="M389 27h83l27 27v111H389Zm83 0v27h27m-89 26h67m-67 22h67m-67 22h42" />
        <path d="M361 202h61v30h-33l-14 13v-13h-14Zm78-12h59v30h-13v12l-15-12h-31Z" />
        <path d="M325 96h32V72h21M326 180h25v23" stroke="#fd4f00" stroke-dasharray="5 5" />
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>Follow the work, check the records and ask the people who know the process.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="practical-checklist">The Practical Warehouse Audit Checklist</h2>
  <p>You can’t fix what you don’t look at. Use these questions to agree the scope with your team and work through it on the floor. Choose the areas relevant to your warehouse; add the specific customer, product or legal requirements your review needs to cover.</p>
  <p>Choose a relevant area below. Open a question to see what to inspect, what to record and how to follow up a concern.</p>
  <div class="audit-checklist" data-audit-checklist>
    <nav class="not-prose audit-area-index font-recoleta" aria-label="Jump to a checklist area">
      <p>Jump to an area in this checklist</p>
      <ul>{areas.map((area, index) => <li><a href={`#audit-area-${index + 1}`}><span>{area}</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></li>)}</ul>
    </nav>
    {areas.map((area, index) => (
      <section class="audit-check-area" id={`audit-area-${index + 1}`} aria-labelledby={`audit-area-title-${index + 1}`}>
        <h3 id={`audit-area-title-${index + 1}`}>{area}</h3>
        <ol class="not-prose audit-checks font-recoleta" start={checkData.checks.findIndex(check => check.area === area) + 1} role="list">
          {checkData.checks.filter(check => check.area === area).map(check => (
            <li class="audit-check" id={`audit-check-${check.id.toLowerCase()}`}>
              <details class="audit-check-guidance" data-audit-guidance open={check === checkData.checks[0]}>
                <summary>
                  <span class="audit-check-number" aria-hidden="true">{checkData.checks.indexOf(check) + 1}.</span>
                  <span class="audit-check-heading"><span class="audit-check-label">{check.label}</span><span class="audit-check-question">{check.question}</span></span>
                  <svg class="audit-disclosure-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
                </summary>
                <div class="audit-check-prompts">
                  <p><strong>Check against</strong> {check.criterion}</p>
                  <p><strong>Look for</strong> {check.evidence}</p>
                  <p><strong>Follow up</strong> {check.nextStep}</p>
                </div>
              </details>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="plan-the-audit">Plan the audit around the decision</h2>
  <p>If you are commissioning the review, tell the reviewer what you need to decide. “Why do these stock differences keep returning?” or “What do we need to resolve before renewing this provider?” gives the work a purpose.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Agree the scope.</strong> Name the site, activities, goods and period to review. Include the busy shifts or unusual conditions that matter to the concern.</li>
    <li><strong>Agree what to check against.</strong> Gather the relevant procedures, service commitments and product requirements. Resolve gaps or disagreements about what was required.</li>
    <li><strong>Arrange access to the evidence.</strong> Identify who can provide the records and explain the work. Agree how the reviewer will report missing or conflicting information.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan the handover.</strong> Decide who will receive the findings, authorise work or resources, and check that actions have worked.</li>
  </ul>
  <figure class="not-prose audit-record-trail" aria-label="From review purpose to verified action">
    <ol>
      {process.map(([title, text]) => <li><strong>{title}</strong><span>{text}</span></li>)}
    </ol>
    <figcaption>If evidence is missing or an action has not worked, return to the investigation.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="execute-the-audit">Executing the audit: Don’t Boil the Ocean</h2>
  <p>Start with a manageable selection of items, transactions or work periods relevant to the concern. Record what you examined so the next person can understand how far the finding extends.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Compare records, direct observation and worker accounts. Keep conflicting accounts visible.</li>
    <li>Include relevant operating conditions in the review, rather than assuming one quiet shift represents every shift.</li>
    <li>For a stock count, consider keeping recorded quantities out of the counter’s view while retaining the identifiers needed to count the right item and unit. <Cite id="1" title="Accurate physical inventory counts" citation="U.S. General Accounting Office (2002)" /></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask workers what happens when they report an exception. At one distribution centre studied by Barratt, Kull and Sodero, workers had to notify a supervisor when there was insufficient stock for an order. The researchers described the effect:</p>
  <figure class="audit-quotation">
    <blockquote><p>This policy impaired employee productivity and, thus, reduced their productivity bonuses.</p></blockquote>
    <figcaption class="font-recoleta"><span class="audit-quote-source">— Barratt, Kull and Sodero (2018)</span><span class="audit-quote-locator">Shortage-reporting policy at the studied distribution centre, manuscript page 17. <Cite id="2" title="Employees and inventory record accuracy" citation="Barratt, Kull & Sodero (2018)" /></span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>In your own review, ask whether the way performance is measured makes an exception difficult to report. Compare what workers describe with the procedure and the records.</p>

  <h2 id="financial-controls">Stock discrepancies and financial controls</h2>
  <p>When a count differs from the record, keep the count notes and investigate the transactions around it. A balance adjustment should leave a record of who authorised it and what still needs explaining. Damaged goods, goods held for someone else and the value recorded for them may need further review with the responsible team.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose audit-record-trail">
    <p class="audit-kicker">Follow one item through the record</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Receipt</strong><span>Confirm the item and unit.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Stock record</strong><span>Identify the location and recorded balance.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Movements</strong><span>Check what changed around the count.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Count and recount</strong><span>Retain the observed quantity and timing.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Adjustment</strong><span>Record the authority and any unresolved cause.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption>A missing connection is an evidence request, not a reason to guess.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>A Martin County public audit recommended a separate verification count where a variance exceeded management’s tolerance:</p>
  <figure class="audit-quotation">
    <blockquote><p>The verification count should be performed by a staff member who did not participate in the original count.</p></blockquote>
    <figcaption class="font-recoleta"><span class="audit-quote-source">— Martin County warehouse audit (2021)</span><span class="audit-quote-locator">Verification counts, recommendation 4, page 24. <Cite id="3" title="Utilities warehouse inventory audit" citation="Martin County Clerk & Comptroller (2021)" /></span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>Example: 11 counted, 12 recorded</h3>
  <p>Suppose the team counts 11 units in a bin and the inventory record shows 12. A recount confirms 11. The team now needs to establish whether the record and count describe the same item, handling unit and point in time.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check what could explain the difference.</strong> Compare receipts, picks and transfers around the count. Check similar product codes and any change between cases and individual units. Record which explanations the evidence supports and which remain open.</li>
    <li><strong>Give the investigation an owner.</strong> The inventory lead follows up the unanswered points. Record any authorised adjustment alongside the investigation, including the evidence used for each.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the next result.</strong> If the same difference keeps returning, use the investigation to choose a corrective action and agree how a later check will show whether it worked.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="compliance-and-customer-audits">Compliance and customer audits</h2>
  <p>If you are preparing for a customer’s audit, ask for the scope and evidence request before gathering documents. Confirm which goods, activities and dates the review covers, which requirements apply and who can answer questions about them.</p>
  <p>United Warehouse describes customers bringing their own reviews into the operation:</p>
  <figure class="audit-quotation">
    <blockquote><p>We are routinely audited by many of our customers, and even by their customers.</p></blockquote>
    <figcaption class="font-recoleta"><span class="audit-quote-source">— United Warehouse</span><span class="audit-quote-locator">Customer-audit account in <em>Warehouse Facility Audits.</em> <Cite id="4" title="Warehouse facility audits" citation="United Warehouse" /></span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The checklist helps you organise that preparation. Attach the actual requirement and supporting evidence to each relevant question, and agree who will deal with any gap the reviewer identifies.</p>
  <h3>Example: records in a UK customs warehouse</h3>
  <p>HM Revenue &amp; Customs (HMRC) is the UK’s tax and customs authority. In a closing stock balance arrangement approved by HMRC, commercial records and the duty-management system together form the customs warehouse stock account. Consignment details, quantities and removal dates must be recorded before goods leave. The duty-management records must then be updated as soon as information is available, before midnight of the following warehouse operation day, with an audit trail linking the records. <Cite id="5" title="Customs warehouse stock records" citation="HM Revenue & Customs (2025)" /></p>
  <p>For that review, take a selected removal and check when it was entered in each record and how the entries connect. Agree the scope with the person responsible for the customs warehouse so the review checks the requirements of its approved arrangement.</p>

  <h2 id="findings-and-actions">Turn findings into decisions</h2>
  <p>A finding should tell the next person what happened, what it was checked against, why it matters and what needs to happen next. Keep the supporting evidence with it so an action owner or director can act on more than a description of the problem.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>State the finding and its limits.</strong> Record the requirement, what you observed and the items or periods examined. Include missing or contradictory evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Make the next step clear.</strong> Name the action owner, due date and any approval, resource or specialist input needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Agree how it will be checked.</strong> Record who will verify the result, when and what evidence they will need.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Example: reviewing a warehouse provider before renewal</h3>
  <p>Suppose dispatches from a 3PL have been late, a customer has questioned the storage conditions and some warehouse charges are disputed. A director needs the review to explain what each concern means for the renewal decision.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Late dispatches.</strong> Compare the agreed dispatch commitment with order, dispatch and handover records for the affected period. Examine staffing, queues and other operating conditions around the delays. Ask the operations lead to report what is established, what needs investigating and any resource decision required.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage conditions.</strong> Confirm the conditions required for the affected goods and request the storage records for the relevant dates. Check which goods, activities and period any assurance report covers. Ask the responsible quality or product specialist to identify the gaps and the evidence needed to answer the customer.</li>
    <li><strong>Disputed charges.</strong> Take a disputed invoice line back to the contract, billed unit and activity record. Have the commercial owner record the calculation, supporting evidence and any correction or unresolved query.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The renewal brief now has three findings with evidence and named owners. Ask the director which unanswered points must be resolved before renewal and who will obtain that evidence. Record the invoice decision, service investigation and storage finding separately so the renewal decision can take all three into account.</p>

  <h2 id="audit-frequency">How often should you audit?</h2>
  <ul><li><strong>Routine checks:</strong> follow the requirements for the task.</li><li><strong>Planned reviews:</strong> choose a scope and interval informed by the operation and its obligations.</li><li><strong>Triggered reviews:</strong> respond to a material change, recurring discrepancy or incident.</li><li><strong>Follow-up checks:</strong> test whether an action worked.</li></ul>
  <p>These are different purposes, not a universal monthly or quarterly schedule.</p>

  <h2 id="technology-and-people">Technology and the people doing the work</h2>
  <p>Records, photographs and warehouse systems can help a reviewer trace what happened. They still need a clear scope, the relevant requirements and someone who can explain how the work is done.</p>
  <p>A useful audit leaves people with an understandable finding, a practical next step and the support to carry it out. The aim is to make sure the system supports the work they do.</p>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ol class="not-prose pl-3">
    <BibliographyItem id="1"><strong>U.S. General Accounting Office (2002).</strong> <a href="https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-02-447g.pdf">Best Practices in Achieving Consistent, Accurate Physical Counts of Inventory and Related Property.</a> GAO-02-447G, Key Factor 8, pp. 39–41. Guidance on blind counting, including its context and alternative controls.</BibliographyItem>
    <BibliographyItem id="2"><strong>Barratt, Kull and Sodero (2018).</strong> <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328417219_Inventory_Record_Inaccuracy_Dynamics_and_the_Role_of_Employees_within_Multi-Channel_Distribution_Center_Inventory_Systems">Inventory record inaccuracy dynamics and the role of employees within multi-channel distribution center inventory systems.</a> Journal of Operations Management, 63, pp. 6–24. The quoted passage is on p. 17 of the author manuscript.</BibliographyItem>
    <BibliographyItem id="3"><strong>Martin County Clerk of the Circuit Court and Comptroller (2021).</strong> <a href="https://www.martinclerk.com/DocumentCenter/View/856/Utilities-Warehouse-Audit-Report---2021-PDF#page=24">Utilities Warehouse Inventory.</a> Audit 2021-A-0001, p. 24, Recommendation 4.</BibliographyItem>
    <BibliographyItem id="4"><strong>United Warehouse.</strong> <a href="https://www.unitedwarehouse.com/why-united/quality-assurance/audits/">Warehouse Facility Audits.</a> Operator self-description, customer-audits section.</BibliographyItem>
    <BibliographyItem id="5"><strong>HM Revenue &amp; Customs (2025).</strong> <a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/special-procedure-customs-warehousing/duty-management-and-closing-stock-balance-systems">Duty management and closing stock balance systems.</a> Guidance for the stated HMRC customs-warehousing context.</BibliographyItem>
  </ol>

</BlogPostLayout>


<style>
  /* Only article-body components. Fonts, prose, title, columns and navigation
     continue to come from the normal BlogPostLayout. */
  .audit-small { font-size: .875rem; line-height: 1.6; }
